This thread is really getting silly. It's the same arguments refuted over and over again. I think we need some illustration to explain why this is intractable.

So here is what it would take, in a NixOS module, to support a really simple service (postgrest) with both `systemd` and `finit`, using a hypothetical shared abstraction to deduplicate as much work as possible:

```nix
{ config, ... }: {
  portable.services.postgrest = {
    description = "PostgREST";
    networked = true;

    # Note the lack of detail compared to
    # systemd - this will be an issue for a lot
    # of services.
    serviceDependencies = [
      "postgresql"
    ];

    script = ''
      if [ -f "$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/pgpass" ]; then
        cp -f "$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY/pgpass" "$CACHE_DIRECTORY/pgpass"
      fi
      exec ${lib.getExe pkgs.postgrest} ${configFile}
    '';
  };

  systemd.services.postgrest.serviceConfig = {
    CacheDirectory = "postgrest";
    CacheDirectoryMode = "0700";
    Environment =
      lib.optional (cfg.pgpassFile != null) "PGPASSFILE=%C/postgrest/pgpass"
      ++ lib.optional (cfg.jwtSecretFile != null) "PGRST_JWT_SECRET=@%d/jwt_secret";
    LoadCredential =
      lib.optional (cfg.pgpassFile != null) "pgpass:${cfg.pgpassFile}"
      ++ lib.optional (cfg.jwtSecretFile != null) "jwt_secret:${cfg.jwtSecretFile}";
    Restart = "always";
    RuntimeDirectory = "postgrest";
    User = "postgrest";

    # Hardening
    CapabilityBoundingSet = [ "" ];
    DevicePolicy = "closed";
    DynamicUser = true;
    LockPersonality = true;
    MemoryDenyWriteExecute = true;
    NoNewPrivileges = true;
    PrivateDevices = true;
    PrivateIPC = true;
    PrivateMounts = true;
    ProcSubset = "pid";
    ProtectClock = true;
    ProtectControlGroups = true;
    ProtectHostname = true;
    ProtectKernelLogs = true;
    ProtectKernelModules = true;
    ProtectKernelTunables = true;
    ProtectProc = "invisible";
    RestrictAddressFamilies = [
      "AF_INET"
      "AF_INET6"
      "AF_UNIX"
    ];
    RestrictNamespaces = true;
    RestrictRealtime = true;
    SystemCallArchitectures = "native";
    SystemCallFilter = [ "" ];
    UMask = "0077";
  };

  finit.services.postgrest = {
    # Yeah, we can't really share the script because
    # we have to reimplement all of `serviceConfig`
    # in bash.
    script = ''
      mkdir -p /run/credentials/postgrest
      systemd-creds decrypt PGRST_JWT_SECRET /run/credentials/postgrest/PGRST_JWT_SECRET
      systemd-creds decrypt pgpass /run/credentials/postgrest/pgpass
      systemd-creds decrypt pgpass /run/credentials/postgrest/PGPASSFILE

      bubblewrap \
        --unshare-all \
        --ro-bind /nix \
        --ro-bind /run/credentials/postgrest \
        # Ok, I can't be bothered further, since
        # bubblewrap doesn't appear to have everything
        # I need.
        #
        # We'd need to add a *bunch* of features to
        # bubblewrap first, and then enumerate them
        # all correctly here, plus all the shim code that
        # systemd normally takes care of.
        ${pkgs.writers.writeBash "postgrest-run" { } config.portable.services.postgrest.script}}

      # Missing features:

      # "Feasible", with a lot of bash
      #
      # CacheDirectory = "postgrest";
      # CacheDirectoryMode = "0700";
      # RuntimeDirectory = "postgrest";
      # CapabilityBoundingSet = [ "" ];
      # DevicePolicy = "closed";
      # DynamicUser = true;
      # LockPersonality = true;
      # ProcSubset = "pid";
      # ProtectHostname = true;
      # ProtectProc = "invisible";
      # UMask = "0077";

      # Needs bubblewrap/finit changes, I think
      #
      # Restart = "always"
      # MemoryDenyWriteExecute = true;
      # ProtectKernelLogs = true;
      # ProtectKernelModules = true;
      # ProtectKernelTunables = true;
      # RestrictAddressFamilies = [
      #   "AF_INET"
      #   "AF_INET6"
      #   "AF_UNIX"
      # ];
      # SystemCallArchitectures = "native";
      # SystemCallFilter = [ "" ];
    '';
}
```

That's one service. Multiply the maintenance effort of this by 10000. This is why @ElvishJerricco says that you'd have to reimplement all of systemd in bash.

It's untested pseudocode, of course. Actually implementing that would probably take days to weeks. I suspect consistent PID detection would go wrong quite often, given how we build scripts to run instead of running the services directly, and that porting existing services will be quite difficult for at least a subset because of it.

If you think my code could be better since I have little finit experience - you're probably right. This applies to 99% of NixOS users, too, so you would end up with the same quality across the board. Likely less, since other contributors are definitely also not fully familiar with all of systemd's intricacies.

Since finit is more limited, the likely outcome would be that new services would use fewer systemd features to limit how much cruft you need to reimplement in bash. The result would be a notable reduction in quality of NixOS' service definitions over time. Multiply that reduction in quality by the number of alternative init systems and the subset of features that can be reasonably implemented with them.

None of this is intended to be commentary on finit or how "good" it is. It seems like a perfectly cromulent init system, just one that isn't easily translated to from systemd (because, yeah, my point is that init systems ***have diverged to the point of being completely incompatible*** ).

Finit doesn't seem to have a trivial equivalent of `ExecStartPre`, `ExecStop*` or `ExecReload` with all their concepts either, which most services rely on over `script` - reimplementing all those in shell code would be quite a feat. Socket activation is absent. There are no file units. The list goes on.

All of those things *could* in theory be reimplemented using bash scripts. Good luck. I don't know how you intend to maintain all of that, especially while keeping up with systemd development adding more features. Also now you've ruined any hope of us ever achieving an interpreter-less system.

---

I honestly doubt this is feasible with sufficient interoperability that all but the most trivial services work. Even *this* one, which I would count among the most trivial services, does not seem feasible without major changes to bubblewrap and a lot of additional bash scripting.

This additionally ignores all of the work to document and explain details of how to use and debug the OS in n different ways, since we no longer have the uniformity of systemd logging and service management (which will certainly make my life on this forum more difficult).

I appreciate all the motivation and desire for this, but it simply is not a reasonable thing to even consider. Build a new distro, like sixos or finix, it will be much easier and way more useful than trying to change NixOS.

Or if you insist on adding this to NixOS, you can start by adding all of systemd's cgroup features to bubblewrap, since that's a requirement to even start this in NixOS.
